  "raw_response": "def solve(grid):\n    # Find rectangle bordered by repeated value\n    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    \n    # Find bounding box of the rectangle marker\n    # Look for a rectangular border made of a single non-standard value\n    # From examples: 2s, 4s, 8s, 3s form the border\n    \n    # Find the border by looking for rows/cols with repeated special values forming a rectangle\n    # Strategy: find top-left and bottom-right of the border\n    \n    top = bot = left = right = -1\n    border_val = -1\n    \n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            # Check if this could be top-left of border\n            # Look for horizontal line of same value\n            pass\n    \n    # Alternative: scan for the rectangular border pattern\n    # The border is a full rectangle outline made of one value\n    for r in range(rows):\n        counts = {}\n        for c in range(cols):\n            v = grid[r][c]\n            counts[v] = counts.get(v, 0) + 1\n        # A border row would have many consecutive same values\n        for v, cnt in counts.items():\n            if cnt >= 4:\n                # Check if they're consecutive\n                positions = [c for c in range(cols) if grid[r][c] == v]\n                if positions[-1] - positions[0] == len(positions) - 1:\n                    if top == -1:\n                        top = r\n                        border_val = v\n                        left = positions[0]\n                        right = positions[-1]\n                    else:\n                        if v == border_val and positions[0] == left and positions[-1] == right:\n                            bot = r\n\n    # Extract interior\n    result = []\n    for r in range(top + 1, bot):\n        row = []\n        for c in range(left + 1, right):\n            row.append(grid[r][c])\n        result.append(row)\n    \n    return result",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    # Find rectangle bordered by repeated value\n    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    \n    # Find bounding box of the rectangle marker\n    # Look for a rectangular border made of a single non-standard value\n    # From examples: 2s, 4s, 8s, 3s form the border\n    \n    # Find the border by looking for rows/cols with repeated special values forming a rectangle\n    # Strategy: find top-left and bottom-right of the border\n    \n    top = bot = left = right = -1\n    border_val = -1\n    \n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            # Check if this could be top-left of border\n            # Look for horizontal line of same value\n            pass\n    \n    # Alternative: scan for the rectangular border pattern\n    # The border is a full rectangle outline made of one value\n    for r in range(rows):\n        counts = {}\n        for c in range(cols):\n            v = grid[r][c]\n            counts[v] = counts.get(v, 0) + 1\n        # A border row would have many consecutive same values\n        for v, cnt in counts.items():\n            if cnt >= 4:\n                # Check if they're consecutive\n                positions = [c for c in range(cols) if grid[r][c] == v]\n                if positions[-1] - positions[0] == len(positions) - 1:\n                    if top == -1:\n                        top = r\n                        border_val = v\n                        left = positions[0]\n                        right = positions[-1]\n                    else:\n                        if v == border_val and positions[0] == left and positions[-1] == right:\n                            bot = r\n\n    # Extract interior\n    result = []\n    for r in range(top + 1, bot):\n        row = []\n        for c in range(left + 1, right):\n            row.append(grid[r][c])\n        result.append(row)\n    \n    return result",
